\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating generalization along seven axes: four in detail below---classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and preliminary cross-scale observations (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale})---and three in the Appendix (end-to-end learning, temperature robustness, and cross-boundary transfer).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates three classifier families---bagging (RF), gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM)---on the same 19 features across three atlas cells spanning the full accuracy range.
Per-cell spread is $\leq$0.4pp, establishing a feature-determined ceiling independent of the learning algorithm.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match the within-cell RF of Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} within 0.1\,pp (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected); all feature-based methods significantly outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%, $p<0.001$) and logistic regression (75.0\%).

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
As a complementary ablation, we test whether depth can be estimated from surface text alone---without the targeted distributional features our pipeline extracts.
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); two zero-shot LLMs---DeepSeek V3.2 (36.2\%, 95\% CI $[32.0, 40.4]$) and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (30.4\%, $[26.5, 34.6]$; each 500 samples, prompt template in Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt})---both fail to distinguish recursive depth, performing near chance (25\%).

Our contributions are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Task formalization and benchmark.} We define recursive generation depth estimation as ordinal classification, introduce the EDD metric, and construct a 180K-sample benchmark spanning 3 model families $\times$ 3 decoding strategies $\times$ 4 depths.

    \item \textbf{Depth Degradation Atlas.} A systematic $3 \times 3$ empirical characterization reveals that perplexity tail statistics as a feature group dominate depth discrimination (top-2 in 9/9 and 8/9 cells), decoding strategy reshapes degradation trajectories qualitatively, and depth discrimination persists through $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}{=}4$ for the best-characterized family (Pythia, $p{=}0.001$; consistent direction across all three families with graded statistical support).

    \item \textbf{Generalization across seven axes (four in main text; three in appendix).} We demonstrate robustness beyond the training grid: cross-model recursive chains (84.75\%---preliminary evidence consistent with a process-level depth signal), preliminary cross-scale observations on two 7B architectures (70.2\% and 60.1\%, with divergent AUC trajectories), cross-domain transfer to arXiv (74.1\%), temperature robustness ($\tau \in [0.5, 0.9]$), and reference model independence ($\Delta{=}4$\,pp). A surface-text ablation (zero-shot LLMs at 30--36\%, 42\,pp below RF) confirms that depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text alone---while requiring only 19 features and a Random Forest for millisecond-scale, GPU-free inference.
\end{itemize}

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating generalization along seven axes: four in detail below---classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and preliminary cross-scale observations (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossscale})---and three in the Appendix (end-to-end learning, temperature robustness, and cross-boundary transfer).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates three classifier families---bagging (RF), gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM)---on the same 19 features across three atlas cells spanning the full accuracy range.
Per-cell spread is $\leq$0.4pp, establishing a feature-determined ceiling independent of the learning algorithm.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match the within-cell RF of Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} within 0.1\,pp (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected); all feature-based methods significantly outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%, $p<0.001$) and logistic regression (75.0\%).

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
As a complementary ablation, we test whether depth can be estimated from surface text alone---without the targeted distributional features our pipeline extracts.
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); two zero-shot LLMs---DeepSeek V3.2 (36.2\%, 95\% CI $[32.0, 40.4]$) and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (30.4\%, $[26.5, 34.6]$; each 500 samples, prompt template in Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt})---both fail to distinguish recursive depth, performing near chance (25\%).
The 42\,pp gap between RF (78.2\%) and both LLMs confirms that the depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text alone; this gap exceeds the CI half-width (${\approx}$4\,pp) by an order of magnitude, ensuring robustness at $n{=}500$.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Classifier convergence: 4-class accuracy (\%) across three atlas cells. RF (bagging), XGBoost and LightGBM (gradient boosting) operate on the same 19 features; $\Delta_{\max}$: largest per-cell spread. Chance = 25\%. RF values differ from Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} by $\leq$0.7pp due to matched-fold evaluation.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Cell & RF & XGB & LGBM & $\Delta_{\max}$ \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc. & 78.8 & 79.0 & 78.9 & 0.2 \\
GPT-2~XL, $\tau$ & 85.8 & 86.2 & 86.2 & 0.4 \\
Pythia, top-$k$ & 68.9 & 68.9 & 68.7 & 0.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Convergence across five structurally different classifiers---spanning bagging, gradient boosting, ordinal regression, and ensemble detection---confirms the signal resides in the features, not the learning algorithm.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread across d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d2 drops to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).
Excluding d0, d1--d3 balanced accuracy averages 55.4\% (chance = 33\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

We train on two decoding strategies and test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%).
d2 recall varies sharply: nucleus holdouts retain 30--54\%, top-$k$ holdouts fall to 8--19\%, and temperature holdouts span 3.7--39.5\% (median 8.9\%).
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature inverts the d1/d2 boundary (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5); per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers it but leaves a residual gap (Limitations).
A multi-temperature ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:multi_tau}) confirms that within-$\tau$ depth signal holds for $\tau \leq 0.9$ (d1v2 AUC $>0.80$) but collapses at $\tau{=}1.1$; cross-$\tau$ LOO transfer (mean 46.7\%) is harder than cross-strategy transfer.
Temperature variations ($\tau \in [0.5, 0.9]$) do not fundamentally alter depth discriminability within a fixed strategy; classification accuracy remains within 3pp of the $\tau{=}0.7$ baseline across all tested temperatures. Full multi-$\tau$ analysis is provided in Appendix~\ref{app:multi_tau}.

End-to-end PLMs (DeBERTa-v3, RoBERTa) achieve only ${\sim}$59\%, lagging RF by 19pp, confirming depth signal resides in perplexity tail statistics inaccessible to standard PLM representations (Appendix~\ref{app:e2e}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

The preceding analyses use 1--1.5B parameter models.
To test whether depth signal persists in substantially larger generators, we extend the benchmark to two 7--8B models: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct~\cite{qwen2.5} and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct~\cite{llama3}, both under 4-bit NF4 QLoRA fine-tuning, generating depths $d \in \{0, 1, \ldots, 5\}$ with nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$, 500 samples per depth).
Feature extraction uses the same reference model (Pythia-1.4B) and 19-feature pipeline as all prior experiments.

\paragraph{Scale comparison.}
Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale} contrasts the two 7--8B models with the three 1--1.5B models on matched evaluation (nucleus sampling, depths 0--3).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-scale comparison: 4-class RF accuracy (\%, depths 0--3, nucleus), $\mathrm{EDD}_{T{=}0.70}$ (depths 0--5), and d0-vs-d1 AUC. CI: 95\% bootstrap. Chance = 25\%.}
\label{tab:cross_scale}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & 4-class acc & $\mathrm{EDD}_{T{=}0.70}$ & d0v1 AUC \\
\midrule
GPT-2~XL (1.5B) & 83.3 & 4 & 0.999 \\
OLMo-1B & 78.8 & 4 & 0.997 \\
Pythia-1.4B & 78.2 & 4 & 0.998 \\
\midrule
Llama-3.1-8B & 70.2\,[68.3, 72.4] & 4 & 0.949 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & 60.1\,[58.0, 62.1] & ${>}5$ & 0.969 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Three findings emerge:

\paragraph{(1) Depth signal persists at 7--8B scale.}
Both 7--8B models substantially exceed the 25\% chance baseline: Llama-3.1-8B achieves 70.2\% (95\% CI $[68.3, 72.4]$) and Qwen2.5-7B achieves 60.1\% ($[58.0, 62.1]$), confirming that recursive depth remains estimable from perplexity-based features even when the generator is 5--7$\times$ larger than the reference model.
Depth-0 separability is preserved in both models: d0v1 AUC is 0.949 (Llama-3.1) and 0.969 (Qwen2.5).

\paragraph{(2) EDD at 7--8B scale.}
At threshold $T{=}0.70$, Qwen2.5-7B maintains all consecutive-pair AUCs above 0.70 through depth~5 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale_auc}), yielding $\mathrm{EDD}_{T{=}0.70} > 5$---exceeding the $\mathrm{EDD}{=}4$ observed at 1--1.5B.
Llama-3.1-8B matches the smaller models at $\mathrm{EDD}{=}4$: its monotonic decay causes d4v5 (0.618) to drop below $T$, despite stronger signal at shallower depths.
Both larger generators produce weaker per-step signal than the 1--1.5B models (mean consecutive AUC: 0.786 for Llama-3.1, 0.782 for Qwen2.5, vs.\ 0.852 at 1--1.5B).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Consecutive binary AUC for 7--8B models (nucleus $p{=}0.95$). Llama-3.1-8B exhibits monotonic decay (d4v5 drops below $T{=}0.70$); Qwen2.5-7B shows a non-monotonic (U-shaped) pattern with all pairs above $T$.}
\label{tab:cross_scale_auc}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & d0v1 & d1v2 & d2v3 & d3v4 & d4v5 \\
\midrule
Llama-3.1-8B & 0.949 & 0.877 & 0.771 & 0.716 & 0.618 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & 0.969 & 0.729 & 0.709 & 0.732 & 0.772 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{(3) Divergent AUC trajectories across architectures.}
Llama-3.1-8B exhibits monotonic AUC decay (0.949 $\to$ 0.877 $\to$ 0.771 $\to$ 0.716 $\to$ 0.618), consistent with the pattern observed at 1--1.5B scale.
In contrast, Qwen2.5-7B shows a U-shaped recovery beyond d2v3: consecutive AUC drops from 0.969 (d0v1) to a minimum of 0.709 (d2v3), then \emph{rises} to 0.732 (d3v4) and 0.772 (d4v5).
This divergence suggests the U-shape observed in Qwen is model-specific---possibly related to its instruction-tuning regime (RLHF/DPO)---rather than a scale-dependent phenomenon.
The Llama-3.1 result demonstrates that monotonic decay \emph{does} replicate at 7--8B scale for at least one architecture, strengthening the generality of the decay pattern as the default behavior.

\paragraph{Caveats.}
For Qwen2.5-7B, the CI lower bound (58.0\%) does not exclude sub-60\% accuracy; Llama-3.1-8B's lower bound (68.3\%) is more comfortably above chance but still below the 75--83\% range at 1--1.5B.
The divergent AUC trajectories across two models caution against strong generalizations from only two 7--8B data points: both the monotonic (Llama-3.1) and non-monotonic (Qwen2.5) patterns are single observations, and further architectures are needed to establish which is the dominant regime at this scale.
Both models are instruction-tuned, introducing a confound absent in the base-model experiments: RLHF/DPO alignment may independently shape the feature trajectory in ways not fully attributable to recursive depth alone.
Cross-model recursive chains (84.75\%) and cross-domain transfer (arXiv 74.1\%) further confirm signal universality (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_boundary}).

\paragraph{Cross-scale confounds.}
Four confounds limit these cross-scale results: (1) 4-bit NF4 QLoRA quantization (absent at 1--1.5B), (2) instruction-tuning (RLHF/DPO, absent in base-model experiments), (3) smaller sample size (500 vs 5{,}000), and (4) evaluation limited to nucleus sampling.

\section{The Depth Degradation Atlas}
\label{sec:atlas}

We present the Depth Degradation Atlas: a systematic empirical map of how statistical fingerprints evolve across recursive generation depths, models, and decoding strategies.
The atlas spans $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells evaluated at depths $d \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$, with depth extensions to $d{=}5$ for cross-model EDD validation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{In-Distribution Performance}
\label{sec:atlas:id}

Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} reports four-class Random Forest accuracy (5-fold CV, 200 trees) across all nine cells.
Accuracy ranges from 69.6\% (Pythia, top-$k$) to 85.4\% (GPT-2~XL, temperature), all exceeding the 25\% chance baseline by a wide margin.
Two axis effects emerge.
GPT-2~XL achieves the highest accuracy under every strategy (76.6--85.4\%), followed by OLMo (74.2--82.0\%) and Pythia (69.6--78.2\%).
Top-$k$ sampling yields the lowest accuracy in every model.
The mean within-model strategy gap is 8.4~pp, comparable to the 7.1~pp mean within-strategy model gap---decoding strategy and model family contribute roughly equally to performance variance.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Four-class RF accuracy (\%) across the $3 \times 3$ atlas, with d1--d3 three-class accuracy in parentheses (chance = 25\% / 33\%). Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all cells.}
\label{tab:main_matrix}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
& Nucleus & Temp.\ $\tau{=}0.9$ & Top-$k$ \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B  & 78.2\,(71.2) & 76.3\,(70.6) & 69.6\,(58.8) \\
OLMo-1B     & 78.8\,(75.0) & 82.0\,(76.3) & 74.2\,(66.1) \\
GPT-2~XL    & 83.3\,(79.0) & 85.4\,(82.6) & 76.6\,(69.5) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-class recall distribution.}
Depth-0 recall exceeds 98\% in all 9 cells, confirming that human-written text is trivially separable from any generation depth.
Among generated depths, d2 consistently yields the lowest recall (46--73\% across cells), followed by d3 (58--82\%) and d1 (72--91\%).
For example, Pythia-1.4B nucleus achieves per-class recall of d0$\,{=}\,$99\%, d1$\,{=}\,$82\%, d2$\,{=}\,$59\%, d3$\,{=}\,$72\%.
The strongest d2 recall is obtained by GPT-2~XL nucleus (73\%).
The d2 trough reflects its position at the center of the depth gradient: d2 features overlap with both the d1 distribution above and d3 below, making it the most confusable class---consistent with the d2-vs-d3 pair being the EDD bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).
Full per-class recall for all cells appears in Appendix~\ref{app:per_class}.

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Feature Consistency Across the Grid}
\label{sec:atlas:features}

Despite the 15.8~pp accuracy spread across cells, a small feature family dominates in every setting.
Perplexity tail statistics as a feature group dominate depth discrimination: the 90th-percentile perplexity (\texttt{p90\_ppl}) ranks top-2 by Gini importance in all 9 cells; surprisal variance (\texttt{var\_surprisal}) in 8/9 under SHAP (7/9 under Gini; Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}).\footnote{Individual feature rankings should be interpreted cautiously given high multicollinearity (VIF${>}$10 for 14--16/19 features); the group-level dominance of perplexity tail statistics is robust across importance measures.}
Table~\ref{tab:feature_importance} shows the full per-cell ranking.

Cross-model feature-rank Spearman correlation averages $\bar{\rho} = 0.868$ (range $[0.619, 0.954]$): models agree on which features carry depth information even when they differ in absolute accuracy.
Among strategies, top-$k$ yields the highest cross-model rank correlation ($\rho = 0.811$)---the deterministic vocabulary truncation imposes a more uniform feature landscape across architectures, at the cost of weaker overall signal (\S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).

Percentile-based features outperform their moment-based counterparts.
Token-level perplexity distributions are heavy-tailed: individual outlier passages can dominate the arithmetic mean of their depth class, whereas \texttt{p90\_ppl} is robust to such extremes while still capturing the tail erosion that signals generation depth.

Feature ablation confirms this concentration: the top-2 features (\texttt{p90\_ppl} + \texttt{var\_surprisal}) alone reach 91.1\% of full accuracy (71.5\%), and the top-5 features achieve 97.6\% (76.5\% vs.\ 78.4\%), with perplexity statistics alone reaching 76.6\% (vs.\ surprisal 73.2\%, other categories ${\leq}$47\%).
Variance inflation analysis reveals VIF${>}$10 for 14--16 of 19 features, placing the effective independent dimensionality at ${\approx}$4--5.
Forward selection independently confirms the dominant pair: \texttt{p90\_ppl} is selected first in 6/9 cells, \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 3/9.

The feature hierarchy also shifts with depth.
At shallow boundaries (d1 vs.\ d2), \texttt{var\_surprisal} dominates importance; at deeper boundaries (d3 vs.\ d4, d4 vs.\ d5), tail perplexity statistics (\texttt{p90\_ppl}, \texttt{kurtosis\_ppl}) take over.
This transition aligns with model-collapse dynamics~\cite{shumailov2024_nature}: early recursion narrows the surprisal distribution (captured by its variance), while later steps erode the perplexity tail (captured by upper quantiles and kurtosis).

